\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Finite-cutoff coercivity and exact normalizers}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{For every #{m_0,\ell,\lambda>0} and odd #{N\ge5}, the
\vocabk{square Dirichlet form}{fdip-00HU} satisfies
#{Q_{\epsilon,D}\ge m_0^2I}. The
\vocabk{half-Dirichlet law}{fdip-00HX} has every absolute polynomial
moment and every exponential moment of a real linear functional.
Writing #{c=c_\epsilon(m_0)} and #{d=N^2}, one has
##{V_\epsilon\ge-\frac{\epsilon^2dc^2}{4},\qquad
0<Z_{\ell,N}\le\exp\left(\frac{\lambda\epsilon^2dc^2}{4}\right).}
The total action obeys, for a finite constant #{K_{\ell,N}},
##{\tfrac12\mathfrak q_{\epsilon,D}(\phi,\phi)
       +\lambda V_\epsilon(\phi)
 \ge\frac{\lambda}{48}\|\phi\|_{L^4(V_{\ell,N},\epsilon^2)}^4
        -K_{\ell,N}.}
For the \vocabk{canonical Euclidean volume}{fdip-002M},
##{\begin{aligned}
\int e^{-\mathfrak q_{\epsilon,D}(\phi,\phi)/2}\,d\operatorname{vol}_{h_\epsilon}
  &=(2\pi)^{d/2}\det(Q_{\epsilon,D})^{-1/2},\\
\int e^{-\mathfrak q_{\epsilon,D}(\phi,\phi)/2-\lambda V_\epsilon(\phi)}
                 \,d\operatorname{vol}_{h_\epsilon}
  &=(2\pi)^{d/2}\det(Q_{\epsilon,D})^{-1/2}Z_{\ell,N}.
\end{aligned}}
No constant in the coercivity or weight bound is asserted uniform in
the mesh.}

\proof{
\p{The incidence term is nonnegative, proving the operator lower
bound. Choose the site-indicator basis. Let #{E_{\mathrm{int}}} contain
internal bonds and #{E_\partial} bonds with one exterior endpoint, each
counted once. Then
##{\mathfrak q_{\epsilon,D}(\phi,\phi)
 =m_0^2\epsilon^2\sum_x\phi_x^2
 +\sum_{\{x,y\}\in E_{\mathrm{int}}}(\phi_x-\phi_y)^2
 +\sum_{(x,y)\in E_\partial}\phi_x^2.}
Every retained site has 4 incident bonds including the exterior ones,
giving the diagonal and neighbor entries in \ref{fdip-00HV}.}

\p{The scalar identity
##{u^4-6cu^2+3c^2=(u^2-3c)^2-6c^2}
gives the stated lower bound on #{V_\epsilon}. Thus
#{0<e^{-\lambda V_\epsilon}\le e^{\lambda\epsilon^2dc^2/4}},
and \ref{fdip-00GI} proves positive finite normalization and all
polynomial moments. A real linear functional has every exponential
moment under a finite-dimensional positive Gaussian. The same bounded
weight estimate proves the assertion for the interacting law.
Splitting the leading quartic into two equal parts absorbs all finite
quadratic coefficients into one part and a constant, leaving the
displayed quartic coercivity bound.}

\p{The Gaussian integral and multiplication by its normalized weight
give both raw integrals by \ref{fdip-00GI}. In the chosen site basis,
#{d\operatorname{vol}_{h_\epsilon}=\epsilon^d\prod_xd\phi_x};
counting-coordinate Lebesgue volume therefore multiplies both raw
integrals by #{\epsilon^{-d}}. The constant term of the Wick quartic
contributes
##{\exp\left(-\frac{\lambda\epsilon^2dc^2}{8}\right)}
to #{Z_{\ell,N}}. It cancels from normalized moments and remains in
the unnormalized integral.}
}

\p{The Gaussian normalization and bounded-weight argument are the
suppliers in \ref{fdip-00GI}; the square-completion and coercivity
estimates above apply them to the Wick interaction of
\citet{(IV.9), (IV.36)}{guerra1975euclideanII}.}
